    {
      "id": "64ca4231-3882-4550-a224-8b749a2aa9d9",
      "name": "Sticky Note2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        0,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 3,
        "width": 768,
        "height": 992,
        "content": "## API Health Monitor & Automated Uptime Alert System\n\nThis n8n workflow completely automates real-time health tracking and incident reporting across standard API endpoints.\n\nRunning dynamically on a schedule, the workflow:\n* Sequentially loops over configured targets, measuring server latencies down to the millisecond.\n* Validates key response conditions (HTTP response codes, response latency ceilings, and explicit body JSON validation).\n* Seamlessly bifurcates downstream logic into distinct operational paths based on healthy vs. unhealthy states.\n* Records audit-ready check details directly within a primary Google Sheets historical ledger.\n* Intelligently references state logs to deduplicate alert messages, silencing redundant pings for existing failures.\n* dispatches instant, descriptive downtime payloads and corresponding service recovery alerts straight to engineers via corporate Slack integrations.\n\nIt converts passive infrastructure monitoring into a proactive, state-aware alert management system.\n\n### Setup Steps\n\n1. Configure the Schedule Trigger  to run the workflow at the desired monitoring interval.\n2. Define the API list with API name, URL, expected status code, and response time threshold.\n3. Loop through each API using the Loop Over Items node for individual processing.\n4. Send an HTTP request to each API and capture the complete response.\n5. Measure the response time to monitor API performance.\n6. Validate the API response by checking the status code, response time, and response body.\n7. Determine the overall health of each API as Healthy or Unhealthy.\n8. Log the monitoring results into Google Sheets for historical tracking.\n9. Generate an alert object for APIs that fail health validation.\n10. Check for existing active alerts to prevent duplicate notifications.\n11. Send alert notifications for newly detected API failures.\n12. Store active alerts to track ongoing incidents.\n13. Monitor API recovery and detect when previously failed APIs become healthy.\n14. Send recovery notifications and clear active alerts after successful recovery.\n15. Repeat the monitoring cycle automatically on every scheduled execution."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
